1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,750
Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array II — LeetCode 80

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:04,600
Problem: nums is sorted; keep each value at most twice, in place, and return k, the new length.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,850
Example: nums = [1, 1, 1, 2, 2] — the third 1 is one copy too many

4
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:11,900
Result: k = 4, nums = [1, 1, 2, 2, …]; each value appears at most twice

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:16,700
Hint: since it's sorted, check the value two slots back — if it matches, you'd be making a third copy

6
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:22,000
Approach: two pointers — f scans, s is the write position; keep nums[f] unless it equals nums[s−2]

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,100
Start: s = write position; each value may be kept at most twice

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,930
f = 0: s < 2, so always keep it

9
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:33,220
f = 1: s < 2, so always keep it

10
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:35,110
f = 2: compare nums[f] with nums[s−2]

11
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:37,750
1 = 1 → that would be a 3rd copy, skip

12
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:39,640
f = 3: compare nums[f] with nums[s−2]

13
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,130
2 ≠ 1 → keep it: advance s and copy

14
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:45,020
f = 4: compare nums[f] with nums[s−2]

15
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:48,510
2 ≠ 1 → keep it: advance s and copy

16
00:00:48,510 --> 00:00:52,350
Loop ends → k = s = 4

17
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:55,900
The first k = 4 elements are the answer — each value at most twice. Time O(n), space O(1)

18
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:01,000
